{"title":"2005 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 16","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Eight spheres of radius 1, one per octant, are each tangent to the coordinate planes. What is the radius of the smallest sphere, centered at the origin, that contains these eight spheres?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/3/c03eb0c51ab801714515476b0041a66bc9baba1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathrm {(A)}\\ \\sqrt{2}  \\qquad \\mathrm {(B)}\\ \\sqrt{3}  \\qquad \\mathrm {(C)}\\ 1+\\sqrt{2}\\qquad \\mathrm {(D)}\\ 1+\\sqrt{3}\\qquad \\mathrm {(E)}\\ 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"465\" height=\"21\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The eight spheres are formed by shifting spheres of radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and center <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d4e5fa16269a1c2026a4a1a9e79fc512b921dd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/f/7cfbd35086ffefb83637d42166582b43c8a1ff4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\pm 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/0/d20258310be3455daf8318274224a9d1d28fb6a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y, z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"44\" height=\"11\" /> directions. Hence, the centers of the spheres are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/0/b80444123176b2b25540dba8da2bb391329bade9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\pm 1, \\pm 1, \\pm 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" />. For a sphere centered at the origin to contain all eight spheres, its radius must be greater than or equal to the longest distance from the origin to one of these spheres. This length is the sum of the distance from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/0/b80444123176b2b25540dba8da2bb391329bade9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\pm 1, \\pm 1, \\pm 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" /> to the origin and the radius of the spheres, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/1/db1a5e916bf39ad89d64ec678eb7184005e4653a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" />. To verify this is the longest length, we can see from the triangle inequality that the length from the origin to any other point on the spheres is strictly smaller. Thus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/0/9304770108cd71ca5c56514980daf0430e861031.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathrm{D}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"25\" height=\"24\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Simplify the problem - in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> dimensions, place <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> circles in each quadrant in the coordinate plane. To find the circle enclosing these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> circles, notice that if you connect the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> centers as a square, the diameter of the large circle will be the diagonal of the square plus two small radii. \n</p><p>Similarly, in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> dimensions, consider the cube created by connecting the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> centers of the spheres. The diameter of the large spheres will just be the space diagonal of this cube, plus the two radiuses on either side. The side length of the cube is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so the space diagonal will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/5/e455d80f4657b1112ba302a6e2c42e9c81f780ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. Then the diameter of the large sphere is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/4/d144a37f7f9a8a676bc21f6b91c7e38031cc077e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{3} + 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" /> and the radius is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/1/db1a5e916bf39ad89d64ec678eb7184005e4653a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt{3} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/0/9304770108cd71ca5c56514980daf0430e861031.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\mathrm{D}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"25\" height=\"24\" />.\n</p><p>~Silverdragon\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/TT5UHS0H2Eo?t=1459\">https://youtu.be/TT5UHS0H2Eo?t=1459</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_16&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B\" title=\"2005 AMC 12B\">2005 AMC 12B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2005 AMC 12B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2005 AMC 12B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2005 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 15\">Problem 15</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_17\" title=\"2005 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 17\">Problem 17</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2005 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2005_AMC_12B_Problems/